The legend:

- `\[AI:<tag>\]` changed while the agent was working, with the run it belongs to. It includes the output of commands the agent ran, such as a formatter.
- `\[AI>HUM:<tag>\]` agent text in a run that the Engine counts as edited while no agent window was open.
- `\[HUM\]` changed while no agent window was open; typing, pasting and other tools are not told apart.
- `\[PST\]` already in the file when recording started; origin not recorded.

### Limits

- The record starts on the day it was installed, and text already in the files is `\[PST\]`.
- `\[HUM\]` means changed while no agent window was open; it cannot tell typing from pasting from other tools.
- [HUM{t=2026-11-21T19:53:24.015Z}]In a session with no prompt events, such as an IDE spec chat that runs tasks, the edits the agent makes between tasks are recorded as `\[HUM\]`.
- While no hook event has been recorded, the Provenance hooks may be disabled, and text changed since setup cannot be attributed to the agent.
- Windows is not supported[AI>HUM:11.2 README section "Reveal Codes for your own repository" 2026-11-21T18:48:45Z.1{t=2026-11-21T18:57:43.165Z}].
[HUM{t=2026-11-21T19:52:49.907Z}]
The full list of limits is in \[the Power's README\](powers/provenance/README.md)[AI>HUM:11.2 README section "Reveal Codes for your own repository" 2026-11-21T18:48:45Z.1{t=2026-11-21T18:57:43.165Z}].

### Install it

Setup writes the hooks into the workspace, because a Power cannot carry them. After setup, the hooks may not be running yet, so check before you rely on the record.

1. Import the Power: in the Powers panel choose Add Custom Power, then Import power from a folder, and select `powers/provenance`.
2. Ask Kiro: "Set up provenance in this workspace". It asks before it writes anything.
3. Check the Agent Hooks panel.
4. Trust the workspace if Kiro asks. On the CLI, use `kiro-cli chat --trust-workspace`: hooks from a folder load only once the folder is trusted.
5. Switch on any Provenance hook that the panel shows as disabled.
6. Reload the window.
7. Send one prompt, and let the setup skill check with `check_records` that a hook event arrived. If none did, the skill says so, and nothing is attributed to the agent until one does.

[PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}]## What leaves your machine

- Verify sends only citation records to this app's own server, and each record includes the case name written before the citation; from there only citation strings go to CourtListener. The rest of your draft text is not sent.
- The saved draft in this browser also holds the case names found for verified citations. Nothing of this is sent anywhere.
- Quotation text is checked in the browser against the opinion and is never sent anywhere.
- Draft, the assistant, sends the prompt and the paragraph at the cursor to the configured LLM provider. Nothing is sent to a provider unless you use Draft. The provider is Amazon Bedrock when `AWS_REGION` and `BEDROCK_MODEL_ID` are set, otherwise Anthropic when `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` are set.
- The checking page at `/check` sends only the citations and the case names written before them, to this site and from there to CourtListener. The rest of the draft stays in the browser. The page does not store the draft, and it is gone when you reload the page. On a screen 800 pixels or wider, opening the draft in the editor stores it there.
- Opening a file reads it in your browser; the file is not uploaded.
- Exporting to DOCX and printing send nothing anywhere; both happen in your browser.
- There is no telemetry.
- A CourtListener token is required for live lookups, because the citation-lookup endpoint answers anonymous requests with 401. Without `COURTLISTENER_TOKEN` the app runs in demo mode on recorded responses and says so in the interface.
- The CourtListener token, if set, is sent only to the CourtListener base URL. It is never cached, logged or sent to the browser.

## Run it locally

Requires Node 22 and pnpm 10.

```
pnpm install
pnpm dev
```

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` to set a CourtListener token or an assistant provider; with no variables set the app runs in demo mode. Other commands: `pnpm lint`, `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm test:pbt`, `pnpm mcp`, `pnpm sentinel`.

The web app builds with webpack (`next dev --webpack`, `next build --webpack`), not Turbopack. The reason, and the settings for hosting it, are in \[docs/DEVELOPMENT.md\](docs/DEVELOPMENT.md).

## How it is built

| Package                      | What it is                                                                                                                   |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `packages/engine`            | The provenance engine: spans, codes, citation extraction, rendering to and parsing from the Reveal Codes form. Pure, no I/O. |
| `packages/verifier`          | CourtListener lookups, opinion text and quotation checks, with request pacing and a cache.                                   |
| `packages/courtlistener-mcp` | The verifier exposed as an MCP server, so the same checks are tools inside Kiro.                                             |
| `packages/export`            | The export gate, the statement, the appendix and the .docx builder; court-specific rules.                                    |
[HUM{t=2026-11-27T00:22:26.537Z}]| `packages/provenance`        | Records which text the agent changed in repository files, and prints a file with its codes. Built on the engine.             |
[PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}]| `apps/web`                   | The Next.js editor: page, codes pane, Issues, assistant, Export and print view.                                              |
| `crew/sentinel`              | The Sentinel CLI that re-checks exported filings.                                                                            |

Because provenance is recorded and not inferred, the engine is deterministic, so its guarantees can be stated as properties and tested: rendering a document to codes and parsing it back returns the same document; an edit never changes the origin of text it did not touch. The suite is [HUM{t=2026-11-27T00:22:26.537Z}]4,897 tests in 463 files: 4,891 run and pass, and 6 are skipped. 214 of them are property-based tests, in 78 files,[PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}] that run at 500 cases each in CI.

## Built with Kiro

Kiro wrote the application code and tests.

| Kiro feature  | In this repository                                                                                                                                                                                                                        [HUM{t=2026-11-27T00:22:26.537Z}]                                                                                                                             |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Specs         | Sixteen, each with requirements, a design with numbered properties, and a task list: provenance-engine, citation-verifier, editor-app, export-certification, shell-polish, sentinel-sweep, court-orders, first-run-and-flow, legal-record-ui, citation-check-page, case-match, court-rules-page, open-draft, pasted-text-declaration, kiro-provenance, recorded-sample |
| Steering      | Seven files: product, structure, tech, testing, UI rules, citation handling, release runbook                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| Hooks         | Eight files: one commits each finished spec task, five run the edited package's tests on save, one prompts an agent to re-check the dataset when it changes, one holds the four provenance hooks, which run when a prompt is submitted, when the agent stops, and before and after each spec task                                                                      |
| MCP           | Two servers, both built in this repository: the CourtListener checker and the provenance record                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |
| Powers        | Two: `citation-check` and `provenance`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |
| Custom agents | `release-checker` and `orders-curator`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |
| Skills        | Five: `add-standing-order`, `check-citations`, `court-ai-rules`, `setup-provenance`, `reveal-codes-for-source`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
| Kiro CLI      | Ran the `orders-curator` agent to add dataset entry 41, with the same agent file and skill as the IDE                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| Kiro Crew     | A separate app schedules the Sentinel (see above)                                                                                                                             [PST{t=2026-11-20T03:04:47Z}]                                                                                                                                                                                         |

The full inventory, with the reason each file exists, is in \[docs/KIRO_USAGE.md\](docs/KIRO_USAGE.md). What was built when, what Kiro got wrong, and what fixed it are in \[BUILD_LOG.md\](BUILD_LOG.md).

Working on the repository with Kiro:

- Pick the `release-checker` agent before a release. It is read-only: it runs the runbook checks and prints one table with the HEAD SHA.
- Pick the `orders-curator` agent to add or update one court order in `data/standing-orders.json`, from a `.uscourts.gov` source.
- The `add-standing-order` skill holds the procedure the curator follows; it also activates when you ask to add a standing order.
- `pnpm kiro:check` fails if a file under `.kiro/` is missing from `docs/KIRO_USAGE.md`, or if the table names a path that does not exist.

## Limits

- Reveal Codes is not legal advice. It reports what a court's instrument says and what a database returned; read the source before you file.
- The dataset covers federal instruments only and is a snapshot dated in each entry. Courts revise these often.
- A citation that CourtListener does not hold reads as not found, even if the case exists.
- The free CourtListener tier is rate-limited, and the hosted site limits the checks per visitor that need CourtListener, and a check answered from the cache does not count. A second Verify within a minute can take up to a minute.
- With no assistant provider configured, the assistant returns a recorded reply, and the statement says so.
- The editor is plain text. It does not format captions, italics or indents.
